review_comments.body:
> What I mean, the error message itself makes no sense.
(...)
Why did it expect 17 assertions and got 18?
Those types of failures take significant effort to understand the reason.

It's not that easy to improve the error message here. This comes from the QUnit feature which allows you to state how many assertions you expect to fire in a given test. This matters because of cases like:
```js
QUnit.test( "true provided to the foo callback", function( assert ) {
  assert.expect( 1 );
  foo( function ( result ) {
    assert.strictEqual( result, true, "Result equal to `true`" );
  } );
} );
```
If you expect `foo` to call the callback immediately with a proper value and the value is `false`, you'll get an obvious error. But if there's a bug in `foo` code that makes the callback not invoked at all, the test would normally pass as no assertion failed - but the `assert.expect( 1 )` line prevents that from happening as we do require exactly one assertion.

You can't pass an error message to `assert.expect` as it's generic by definition.

Since what you experienced may be a common omission when working with support tests, we could consider adding one initial test that would check that `expectedMap` has the same number of keys as `computedSupport` - then we can provide an error message to that assertion as the third parameter to `assert.strictEqual`.

Would you like to try a separate PR with such an improvement? That one would be quite short.

